From ec4936ccc2a3ae2b1b1f590bd9e203da6db59fbf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bench <bench@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 04:18:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 35/65] perf(chonk): parse step bytecode in step order on the
 main thread again

The background bytecode parser (parse step bytecode on a background thread)
raised peak RSS: unbounded, it kept every step's parsed program alive at once
(+64 MiB on transfer_0, +123 MiB on storage_proof); bounded to one step ahead,
the parses still land in the worker thread's malloc arena, which the prover's
allocations on the main thread cannot reuse (+4.9% peak RSS summed over five
flows), for a 1.0% wall-time gain on 4 cores. Client proving is memory-bound,
so the CLI parses each step's bytecode right before accumulating it again,
through ChonkLoad as the bbapi path does. Bytecode, witness and VK are moved
out of the loaded steps rather than copied, and ChonkLoad is back to its
upstream form.
